How is this different from `deepStrictEqual(...)` and `deepEqual(...)`? The docs should expand on that more. As it is, these examples do not really provide enough detail to determine the correct one to choose. IMO these tests can be simplifies using a `for` loop thanks for the update! I adjusted the docs now @RedYetiDev looking at the end result, I am not sure it is cleaner or not, but I did it anyway 😄  The naming here might be confusing given that `assert.match(...)` exists and uses regexes but this `deepMatch()` has nothing to do with regexes.

It's a bit more verbose both something like `assert.partialDeepEqual()` and `assert.partialDeepStrictEqual()` might be better / less confusing? I'm less convinced that this one is useful. Why not just simply use `assert(actual.includes(expected))` @jasnell I just followed the most "approved" comment in the original PR and implemented it :) 

https://github.com/nodejs/node/issues/50399#issuecomment-2296531960 @jasnell I just followed the most "approved" comment in the original PR and implemented it :) 

https://github.com/nodejs/node/issues/50399#issuecomment-2296531960 I think we should use primordials : `ArrayPrototypeEvery` and `ArrayPrototypeSome` Should we avoid `for ... of`? as per https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/b0443690f89816ca3b9b8dee7e73f4527b3ff3e0/doc/contributing/primordials.md#unsafe-array-iteration  Do we need primordials also here? @pmarchini I thought the same but then I looked for the non primordials usage across the codebase and, considering the huge usage of them, I was like "maybe I should not" . Converted tho 😄  FWIW, +1 regarding the naming semantics and avoiding "match". My only alternative suggestion would be `deepEqualSubset()`, which is potentially a bit more intuitive than "partial" (although ES2025 will introduce the Set-specific notion of subset matching, which could itself be a source of confusion). @Renegade334 to be completely honest I like the "partial" more, I have the feeling it is a little more immediate to understand what the method does.  
But, I would like to have a final decision here 😄 is there any way we can tag people to take definitive decision? +1 on not using `match` – and we could discuss having a `deepMatch` for regexes

```js
assert.deepMatch(result, {
  stdout: /Hello/,
  stderr: /World/,
});
```

Maybe a bit niche, but would fit the naming better IMO I suspect this function signature is such that the types of `actual` and `expected` need validation here, otherwise this call to `assertIncludes()` is at risk of generating TypeErrors. Ditto. I believe that null/undefined are already covered by your `isPlainObject()` logic above. I think it'd be worthwhile being more stringent with validating the `expected` errors in the throw tests, at least checking whether the error is an AssertionError, an invalid args error, etc. Does this need to be wrapped in `hideStackFrames()`? done 😄  nit: we don't need to create a throw-away array here
```suggestion

    for (const { 0: key, 1: val } of safeIterator) {
``` same here
```suggestion

    for (const item of safeIterator) {
      if (!SetPrototypeHas(expected, item)) {
``` IMO we should just be using `Array.prototype.includes`, this really should only belong in `partialDeepEqual`. Having `assert.includes` makes perfect sense, having `assert.includesStrict` less so (or it should be an alias maybe).

```suggestion
  return ArrayPrototypeIncludes(actual, expected);
``` As I was saying in the other comment, I don't really like this approach for arrays, IMO it should mirror the result of `assert.ok(actual.includes(expected))`, just like for strings.

```suggestion
assert.includes([1, 2, 3], ['2', 3]);
// AssertionError

assert.includes([1, 2, 3], '2');
// AssertionError

assert.includes([1, 2, 3], 2);
// OK
``` @aduh95 this was intentionally done to comply with https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/b0443690f89816ca3b9b8dee7e73f4527b3ff3e0/doc/contributing/primordials.md#unsafe-array-iteration @aduh95 this was intentionally done to comply with https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/b0443690f89816ca3b9b8dee7e73f4527b3ff3e0/doc/contributing/primordials.md#unsafe-array-iteration I would be kinda agreeing with you if most of the methods didn't have its own `strict` variant. If we would do what you suggested, we would have `includes` which is not doing type coercion, going agains the pattern we have for pretty much the whole `assert` module.

Not changing it for now until I get more people agreeing with you, being the PR almost landing and already having 3 approvals from TSC members I'm well aware of this document, I've written it 😃 `safeIterator` is not an array though, it's a safe iterator, so there's no unsafe array iteration in my suggestion.

https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/daea065f249648a005a9caaa548a76db42eacbb1/doc/contributing/primordials.md#L211-L213 Another path would be to remove `includes` from this PR so `partialDeepStrictEqual` can land, and we can discuss the `includes` (and possibly `includesStrict`) in another PR, wdyt?

