```suggestion
``` These lines are the key lines of this change - it doesn't work otherwise.

They change the internal 'should we automatically add these headers' state for Connection/Content-Length/Transfer-Encoding - if `false`, the headers are added automatically. By default they're set to `false` [here](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/fd9a79df7dcf63990ff15c99433d6d86c95b794e/lib/_http_outgoing.js#L128-L130) in OutgoingMessage, and in the current codebase they're only set to `true` [here](https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/fd9a79df7dcf63990ff15c99433d6d86c95b794e/lib/_http_outgoing.js#L853-L866) if you call `removeHeader(...)` for the corresponding header (as noted in the PR description - it's not possible to call this method in the raw header case).

These lines mean that they're set to `true` up front (overriding the OutgoingMessage default and disabling the automatic inclusion of these headers, as if the 3 `removeHeader(...)` calls had been made) if `setDefaultHeaders: false` is passed in the options.

Does that make sense @jasnell? Ugh, yeah I forgot about that in `OutgoingMessage` these are existing fields. We should see about changing these at some point to internal private fields.

I can try write down a dedicated section for this if you want, I think it'd be worth having (at least to highlight it's experimental) Sure! ````markdown
#### Introducing experimental `assert.partialDeepStrictEqual`

Sometimes, when writing test, we want to validate that some specific properties
are present, and the mere presence of additional keys are not exactly relevant
for that specific test. For this use case, we can now use
`assert.partialDeepStrictEqual`, which should be familiar to those already using
`assert.deepStrictEqual`, with the main difference that does not require all
properties in the `actual` parameter to be present in the `expected` parameter.

Here are a few examples of usage:

```js
assert.partialDeepStrictEqual(
  { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 },
  { a: 1, b: 2 },
);

assert.partialDeepStrictEqual(
  [1, 2, 3, 4],
  [2, 3],
);

assert.partialDeepStrictEqual(
  { a: { b: { c: 1, d: 2 } }, e: 3 },
  { a: { b: { c: 1 } } },
);

assert.partialDeepStrictEqual(
  { a: { b: { c: 1, d: 2 } }, e: 3 },
  { a: { b: { c: 1 } } },
);

assert.partialDeepStrictEqual(
  new Set([{ a: 1 }, { b: 1 }]),
  new Set([{ a: 1 }]),
);

assert.partialDeepStrictEqual(
  { a: new Set([{ a: 1 }, { b: 1 }]), b: new Map(), c: [1, 2, 3] },
  { a: new Set([{ a: 1 }]), c: [2] },
);
```
